Hexagon HTP: the conv chain of a gated delta net layer over a batch of tokens

Base: llama.cpp commit c6824a9 with patches/series applied in order, thru hexagon-fusion/0005.

This patch is the last entry of patches/series, after hexagon-fusion/0005.

Files:
    ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/gdn-conv-ops.c   the batch kernel, and the lane math as shared helpers
    ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/htp-ops.h        the opcode
    ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/main.c           the dispatch
    ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/htp-ctx.h        the prototype
    ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp-gdn-match.h      the chain match, from one token to a batch
    ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/ggml-hexagon.cpp     the opcode of a batch, the name, the slot index
    tests/test-backend-ops.cpp                 the fusion test over a batch

How to apply, from the llama.cpp root:

    git apply patches/hexagon-fusion/0006-hexagon-htp-gdn-conv-chunk.patch
    cmake --build build-snapdragon -j

Push to the phone: libggml-htp-v79.so (ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/), libggml-hexagon.so (bin/) and
test-backend-ops (bin/). The switch is the one of the state fusion, GGML_HEXAGON_OPFUSION_STATE,
which the app sets.

Problem: the conv of a prefill batch moves the tokens twice

A gated delta net layer builds its conv input with a CONCAT that puts the three state columns of the
cache in front of the transposed projection. That CONCAT writes a copy of the whole batch, and the
SSM_CONV then reads that copy. One batch of 256 tokens and 6144 channels is 6.3 MB, thus the pair
moves 25.3 MB where the work needs 12.7 MB. The profile of one pp512 batch of Qwen3.5-2B shows it:
CONCAT 36 ops 25.9 ms and SSM_CONV 36 ops 28.5 ms, 54.4 ms together, and each op runs at 16 to 18
GB/s, which is the bandwidth of the part. The pair is not slow, it is twice as much traffic as the
work needs.

Patches 0002 thru 0004 already fuse that chain at one token, for decode. This patch takes the same
chain over a batch. One op reads the projection once, and writes the conv output and the new state of
the cache:

    GET_ROWS(cache_r, s_copy) -> CONCAT(state, projection^T) -> SSM_CONV -> SILU, and the CPY of the
    last three columns into the slot of the cache        ->   HTP_OP_GDN_CONV_CHUNK

The kernel

A thread owns a range of channels, a multiple of 32, over every token of the batch. Its prologue
turns the weights and the state of those channels into planes, one lane per channel, in VTCM. The
token loop then reads one vector for each of the four taps and writes one vector of outputs, and the
four taps of a token are four rows of the batch, or a state plane at the first three tokens. Thus the
loop never converts a lane layout again, and it runs 20 vector operations for each 32 channels of a
token.

The channels of one token are contiguous in the projection and in the output, thus each thread walks
its own block of each row, and the four rows that one token reads stay in L2 over the channel sweep.
The DDR traffic is one read of the projection and one write of the output.

The new state of the cache is the last three tokens of the batch in the layout of the cache. Three
steps of the single token block of patch 0003, from an empty state, give exactly that, thus the lane
packing of the state has one implementation and one test.

The lane math of patch 0003 is now four helpers (the deal of four vectors, the lanes of a block to
the four tap form, the four taps and the SiLU, and the four tap form back to the lanes of the state).
The single token block is those four helpers in a row, thus the decode path keeps the instructions
that the phone already runs.

The numbers

The sum of the four taps keeps the order of the CPU reference, and the SiLU is the sigmoid and the
multiply of the SILU op of this backend. Thus the result of the fused op is the result of the four
ops that it replaces, bit for bit, which the test holds at the tolerance of an exact kernel.

Expected speed, from the traffic and the measured bandwidth of the part:

    one pp512 batch   54.4 ms today  ->  26 to 28 ms, thus 27 ms of the batch
    one image turn    71 ms today    ->  35 ms, thus 36 ms of the turn (672 tokens)

The compute of one op is 164 us over six threads against 750 us of traffic, thus the op stays at the
bandwidth of the part.

The match

The chain of a batch matches when: the conv width is 4, the batch holds at least 4 tokens, the
channel count is a multiple of 32, the CONCAT holds the state columns in front of the batch, the
projection is the transposed view with a stride of one float between channels, and the CPY takes the
last three columns into one row of the cache. Each condition that fails names itself with
GGML_HEXAGON_VERBOSE=1, and the unfused ops run. One token keeps HTP_OP_GDN_CONV_STEP of patch 0003,
thus decode does not change.
